it's chewy stretchy and so delicious  mochi is a sweet or savory rice cake  that's been enjoyed for centuries you  may have had it filled with ice cream  red bean or on its own as a topping  today we're going to show you how to  make daifuku which is just a variation  of mochi that has a sweet filling inside  there are a ton of varieties but we'll  be stuffing ours with three of the most  common fillings red bean paste  strawberries and matcha ice cream okay  time to make some mochi magic  mochi is made from a glutinous rice  called mochigome which is steamed and  then pounded into the smooth chewy  consistency traditionally in japan the  rice is first soaked in water and then  steamed in boxes called seido the  steamed rice is then transferred to a  mortar known as usu and pounded with  huden mallets called kinet a rhythm  forms amongst those pounding the mochi  and the person turning and wetting the  mochi once the pounding is complete the  mochi is ready to be shaped  we know not everyone has gigantic  mallets at home so today we're going to  show you how to make mochi using a sweet  and super easy mochi base called yuhi to  make yuhi we'll need mochiko a sweet  rice flour mochiko is made out of the  same glutinous rice that gives mochi its  amazing texture we'll start by mixing  together our mochiko sugar and water and  a microwave-safe bowl until the mixture  is smooth  to color our strawberry daifuku mochi  pink we're going to add a teaspoon of  dragon fruit powder to the mixture  and to color our matcha ice cream  daifuku green we'll add a teaspoon of  matcha powder  cover the bowl with plastic wrap and  microwave it in 60 second intervals  we're cooking our mochi mixture and a  microwave but you can also use a  stovetop rice cooker or steamer the  mixture should look halfway transparent  like this give it a quick mix cover it  again and then pop it back in the  microwave for 15 to 30 second intervals  and make sure to keep stirring in  between we really want to be careful not  to burn our mochi mixture you'll know  when it's ready when it's super glossy  like this cover your work surface and  cornstarch and pour the mochi mixture  out it's going to be super hot so just  let it sit for a minute to cool down  mochi dough is super sticky so it's  really important that you also dust the  mochi and your hands with cornstarch  next we'll roll out the dough using a  rolling pin until it's half an inch  thick then we'll use a pizza cutter to  cut out 3 by three inch mochi dough  squares you should have four squares per  color  okay let's get into our beautiful  fillings the first filling we're going  to make is red bean paste you can buy  pre-made paste in grocery stores but we  like to make it from scratch to control  its moisture levels  first we'll rinse half a cup of red  beans under cold water we love adzuki  beans from hokkaido because they're  smaller and a bit more tender than the  other kinds then we'll add the beans and  one and a quarter cups of water to a  rice cooker and cook for about an hour  and a half the beans should still be  firm like this we'll rinse the beans one  more time and put them back in the rice  cooker with another half cup of water  we'll cook the bean mixture again for an  hour and a half until the beans are  super tender and cooked through  we'll sweeten it up with some sugar and  salt  and just let it sit in the rice cooker  one last time for about 30 minutes  then we'll mash everything together and  spread the paste onto a small baking  tray to let it cool down you want it to  feel pliable and not too firm like  play-doh if it's too dry refrigerate it  for at least two hours or up to  overnight if it's too wet microwave it  in 90 second intervals until the water  has evaporated cover the red bean paste  with plastic wrap and let it cool to  room temperature we'll use some of this  for our red bean daifuku and some for  our strawberry daifuku to prepare  strawberry daifuku filling we'll cover  four strawberries and red bean paste  start by rolling the red bean paste into  a small ball and then flattening it into  a disc then bring in your beautiful  strawberry and work the red bean paste  from the top down to the bottom of it  like this for fruit filled mochi it's  best to pick ripened delicious fruits  such as strawberries pineapples and  kiwis strawberries are the best for  beginners because they're not as watery  and they hold up their shape well now  we'll cover these and let them chill in  the fridge while we prepare our matcha  ice cream filling preparing your filling  for ice cream mochi is really simple  it's crucial that your ice cream is  super cold so it doesn't melt while  you're trying to wrap it that can get  messy trust me  our trick for this is scooping out the  ice cream in advance and freezing it for  at least an hour to let it really harden  now we're ready to put these cute mochi  babies together we'll start with our red  bean daifuku place one of the mochi  squares in your palm and add two  tablespoons of red bean paste in the  center bring the edges of the dough up  and over to seal like this make sure  your fingers are adequately dusted with  cornstarch so the mochi doesn't stick  blot the seam side down on the dusted  surface and trim off any excess mochi  you can save the extra pieces for a  well-deserved snack now or later that  was easy right let's do it again with  our strawberries for a strawberry  daifuku we'll wrap our strawberries in  our gorgeous pink mochi using the same  technique place a pink square of mochi  in your hand and place a wrapped  strawberry in the center of the mochi  the bottom tip should be pointing down  like this now bring the edges of the  dough up and over to seal like we did  last time again we'll blot the sticky  side on the dusty surface okay i promise  you this will all be worth it now it's  time for a delicious matcha ice cream  daifuku place one of the green mochi  squares in the palm of your hand and  drop a scoop of ice cream in the center  our ice cream is now cold and hard and  much easier to work with connect the  four seams with your other hand and once  it's wrapped up blot the sticky part  again  make sure to set your mochi seam side  down  now we finally get to eat them and  you'll want to eat them right away  because nothing compares to chewy  delicious texture of fresh mochi  whether it's a red bean strawberry or  matcha ice cream filled daifuku we  guarantee each of your cute creations  will be delicious you can package them  in little cupcake holders and give them  as gifts or you can have a little mochi  party and eat them with your family and  friends you can find the recipes for all  three daifuku's on tasty.com we really  hope you love this as mochi as we do  oh yes  [Music]  you
 

